// A write through an `exclusive` borrow must land in the CALLER's object.
//
// WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. The LLVM backend passed the address of a SPILLED COPY
// for every borrow, so `grow(exclusive buf)` incremented a temporary that died
// at the call and the caller's `buf` never changed. Nothing crashed, nothing
// refused, nothing warned: the program printed the wrong number. That is worse
// than a compiler that falls over, and it survived a green gate because the
// gate compared emit VERDICTS between backends (both accepted this) and
// executed four programs, none of which wrote through a borrow. Every silent
// class of defect needs a corpus entry that PRINTS, because a silent program
// cannot catch a wrong answer, and `ownership.cell` and `borrows.cell` are
// both silent by design.
//
// WHAT IT PRINTS, and how to read a wrong answer. One integer, `142`:
//
//   the last two digits   `buf.len`, starting at 37 and incremented once by
//                         each of the five unique-borrow spellings. 42 means
//                         all five wrote through; 41 means exactly one lost
//                         its write; 37 means none of them landed.
//   the hundreds digit    how many DISTINCT addresses the seven calls handed
//                         to the C side. 1 is correct: every spelling passes
//                         the caller's own object. 7 means every call site
//                         made its own copy, which is what the defect did.
//
// So the pre-fix LLVM backend printed `737` and the C backend printed `142`.
// The two `shared` calls are in the count because a shared borrow that copies
// is invisible by value: reading a copy answers the same as reading the
// original, and only the address distinguishes them.
//
// WHY THE MUTATOR HAS NO BODY HERE. Writing `b.len = b.len + 1` in Cell would
// pin only the C and LLVM backends: the MLIR backend refuses assignment
// through a field path today ("cannot lower to MLIR"), and this file would
// then disagree with itself across backends and fail the gate's agreement
// stage for a reason that has nothing to do with what it is testing. Declaring
// `grow` and `look` without bodies moves the mutation to `write_through_host.c`
// and leaves this file testing exactly one thing: which address the CALL SITE
// hands over. All three backends emit that call, so all three are measured.
// The callee half is pinned by a unit test in src/cell/llvmemit.zig that runs
// the Cell-bodied version and expects 42.
//
// EXPECT-OUTPUT: 142

pub struct Buffer { copy len: Int }

pub fn print_int(copy value: Int);

// Defined in write_through_host.c: records the address it was handed, then
// `b->len += 1`. `runtime/cell_rt.h` section 7 and docs/OWNERSHIP.md R1 both
// define `exclusive` as the callee mutating the CALLER's value, so this
// increment is only visible to `main` if the address is `main`'s own.
pub fn grow(exclusive b: Buffer);

// Records the address and reads nothing. `shared` promises the callee will not
// free or mutate, and says nothing about copying, which is exactly why a copy
// here was invisible until the addresses were counted.
pub fn look(shared b: Buffer);

// The number of distinct addresses recorded so far.
pub fn distinct() -> Int;

pub fn main() {
    var owned buf = Buffer { len: 37 }

    // All five unique-borrow spellings, which examples/borrows.cell declares
    // identical. They are NOT identical on the way to a backend: `exclusive
    // buf` arrives as a bare reference because the parser's written prefix is
    // consumed during lowering, while the four sigil forms arrive wrapped in a
    // unary borrow node. A backend that handles one and not the others splits
    // the group silently, and the arithmetic above is what makes that visible.
    grow(exclusive buf)
    grow(&mut buf)
    grow(&var buf)
    grow(&exclusive buf)
    grow(exclusive &buf)

    // Both shared spellings, for the same reason.
    look(shared buf)
    look(&buf)

    print_int(buf.len + 100 * distinct())
}
